    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "n = 4, flights = [[0,1,100],[1,2,100],[2,0,100],[1,3,600],[2,3,200]], src = 0, dst = 3\n\nk = 1  ->  700    (0 -> 1 -> 3)\nk = 2  ->  400    (0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3)\nk = 0  ->  -1     (no direct flight from 0 to 3)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: shortest path with a limit"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Without the limit, this is Dijkstra. With it, Dijkstra's key promise breaks: Dijkstra **settles** a city at its cheapest price and never looks back — but the cheapest way into a city may use too many flights, while a pricier way with fewer flights is the one that can still reach `dst` in time. In the picture, city 2 is cheapest via 1 (200), but a route through 2 then needs 2 stops."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Bellman-Ford** handles limits naturally because it counts edges. Round 1 finds the cheapest price to every city using **one** flight; round 2, using **at most two**; and so on. Run exactly `k + 1` rounds and read off `dst`. One detail makes it exact: during a round, relax from the **previous** round's prices (a copy), otherwise a single round could chain two flights."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Anytime a path is limited by a count — stops, fuel, discounts, walls you may break — the trick is the same: make the count part of the **state** (or the round number), so routes with different counts are kept apart."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: try every route"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A DFS tries every sequence of at most `k + 1` flights from `src`, remembering the cheapest that ends at `dst`. Stop following a route once it already costs as much as the best one."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function findCheapestPrice(n, flights, src, dst, k) {\n  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);\n  for (const [u, v, price] of flights) adj[u].push([v, price]);\n  let best = Infinity;\n  const fly = (city, spent, flightsLeft) => {\n    if (spent >= best) return;              // already no cheaper than the best route\n    if (city === dst) {\n      best = spent;\n      return;\n    }\n    if (flightsLeft === 0) return;\n    for (const [next, price] of adj[city]) fly(next, spent + price, flightsLeft - 1);\n  };\n  fly(src, 0, k + 1);                       // k stops = at most k + 1 flights\n  return best === Infinity ? -1 : best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def find_cheapest_price(n, flights, src, dst, k):\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v, price in flights:\n        adj[u].append((v, price))\n    best = float(\"inf\")\n\n    def fly(city, spent, flights_left):\n        nonlocal best\n        if spent >= best:\n            return                        # already no cheaper than the best route\n        if city == dst:\n            best = spent\n            return\n        if flights_left == 0:\n            return\n        for nxt, price in adj[city]:\n            fly(nxt, spent + price, flights_left - 1)\n\n    fly(src, 0, k + 1)                    # k stops = at most k + 1 flights\n    return -1 if best == float(\"inf\") else best"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<List<int[]>> adj;\nint best, dst;\n\nint findCheapestPrice(int n, int[][] flights, int src, int dst, int k) {\n    adj = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    for (int[] f : flights) adj.get(f[0]).add(new int[]{f[1], f[2]});\n    this.dst = dst;\n    best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    fly(src, 0, k + 1);                          // k stops = at most k + 1 flights\n    return best == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : best;\n}\n\nvoid fly(int city, int spent, int flightsLeft) {\n    if (spent >= best) return;                   // already no cheaper than the best route\n    if (city == dst) {\n        best = spent;\n        return;\n    }\n    if (flightsLeft == 0) return;\n    for (int[] f : adj.get(city)) fly(f[0], spent + f[1], flightsLeft - 1);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "void fly(vector<vector<pair<int, int>>>& adj, int city, int dst, int spent, int flightsLeft, int& best) {\n    if (spent >= best) return;                   // already no cheaper than the best route\n    if (city == dst) {\n        best = spent;\n        return;\n    }\n    if (flightsLeft == 0) return;\n    for (auto [next, price] : adj[city]) fly(adj, next, dst, spent + price, flightsLeft - 1, best);\n}\n\nint findCheapestPrice(int n, vector<vector<int>>& flights, int src, int dst, int k) {\n    vector<vector<pair<int, int>>> adj(n);\n    for (auto& f : flights) adj[f[0]].push_back({f[1], f[2]});\n    int best = INT_MAX;\n    fly(adj, src, dst, 0, k + 1, best);          // k stops = at most k + 1 flights\n    return best == INT_MAX ? -1 : best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(dᵏ⁺¹), space O(k).** d is the most flights leaving one city: each level of the search can branch d ways, k + 1 levels deep. Fine for tiny k, hopeless for k = 100."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Bellman-Ford, k + 1 rounds"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Keep `cost[city]`, the cheapest price found so far. Each round copies it into `next`, relaxes every flight from `cost` into `next`, and then makes `next` the new `cost`. After round i, `cost` is exact for routes of at most i flights."
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "cheapest-flights",
      "caption": "Each round allows one more flight. Compare k = 1 with k = 2 on the same map."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function findCheapestPrice(n, flights, src, dst, k) {\n  let cost = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);\n  cost[src] = 0;\n  for (let round = 0; round <= k; round++) {   // k stops = at most k + 1 flights\n    const next = [...cost];                 // relax from LAST round's prices only\n    for (const [u, v, price] of flights) {\n      if (cost[u] + price < next[v]) next[v] = cost[u] + price;\n    }\n    cost = next;                            // now: cheapest using at most round + 1 flights\n  }\n  return cost[dst] === Infinity ? -1 : cost[dst];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def find_cheapest_price(n, flights, src, dst, k):\n    cost = [float(\"inf\")] * n\n    cost[src] = 0\n    for _ in range(k + 1):                # k stops = at most k + 1 flights\n        nxt = cost[:]                     # relax from LAST round's prices only\n        for u, v, price in flights:\n            if cost[u] + price < nxt[v]:\n                nxt[v] = cost[u] + price\n        cost = nxt                        # now: cheapest using one more flight\n    return -1 if cost[dst] == float(\"inf\") else cost[dst]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int findCheapestPrice(int n, int[][] flights, int src, int dst, int k) {\n    final int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    int[] cost = new int[n];\n    Arrays.fill(cost, INF);\n    cost[src] = 0;\n    for (int round = 0; round <= k; round++) {       // k stops = at most k + 1 flights\n        int[] next = cost.clone();                   // relax from LAST round's prices only\n        for (int[] f : flights) {\n            int u = f[0], v = f[1], price = f[2];\n            if (cost[u] != INF && cost[u] + price < next[v]) next[v] = cost[u] + price;\n        }\n        cost = next;                                 // now: cheapest using one more flight\n    }\n    return cost[dst] == INF ? -1 : cost[dst];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int findCheapestPrice(int n, vector<vector<int>>& flights, int src, int dst, int k) {\n    const int INF = INT_MAX;\n    vector<int> cost(n, INF);\n    cost[src] = 0;\n    for (int round = 0; round <= k; round++) {       // k stops = at most k + 1 flights\n        vector<int> next = cost;                     // relax from LAST round's prices only\n        for (auto& f : flights) {\n            int u = f[0], v = f[1], price = f[2];\n            if (cost[u] != INF && cost[u] + price < next[v]) next[v] = cost[u] + price;\n        }\n        cost = next;                                 // now: cheapest using one more flight\n    }\n    return cost[dst] == INF ? -1 : cost[dst];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(k · E), space O(n).** k + 1 rounds, each reading all E flights once, plus a copy of the n prices per round."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Dijkstra on (city, flights used)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Dijkstra can work too, if the state includes how many flights were used. Pop the cheapest `[cost, city, used]`. The first time `dst` comes out, that's the answer. Otherwise expand the city — but only if this pop used **fewer** flights than any earlier pop of the same city: an earlier pop was cheaper, so a later one is only useful if it has flights to spare."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function findCheapestPrice(n, flights, src, dst, k) {\n  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);\n  for (const [u, v, price] of flights) adj[u].push([v, price]);\n  const fewest = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);    // fewest flights of any earlier pop of each city\n  const heap = new Heap((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);  // [cost, city, flights used], cheapest first\n  heap.push([0, src, 0]);\n  while (heap.size() > 0) {\n    const [cost, city, used] = heap.pop();\n    if (city === dst) return cost;          // the cheapest route within the limit\n    if (used >= fewest[city] || used === k + 1) continue;   // dominated, or out of flights\n    fewest[city] = used;\n    for (const [next, price] of adj[city]) heap.push([cost + price, next, used + 1]);\n  }\n  return -1;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "import heapq\n\ndef find_cheapest_price(n, flights, src, dst, k):\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v, price in flights:\n        adj[u].append((v, price))\n    fewest = [float(\"inf\")] * n           # fewest flights of any earlier pop of each city\n    heap = [(0, src, 0)]                  # (cost, city, flights used), cheapest first\n    while heap:\n        cost, city, used = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if city == dst:\n            return cost                   # the cheapest route within the limit\n        if used >= fewest[city] or used == k + 1:\n            continue                      # dominated, or out of flights\n        fewest[city] = used\n        for nxt, price in adj[city]:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (cost + price, nxt, used + 1))\n    return -1"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int findCheapestPrice(int n, int[][] flights, int src, int dst, int k) {\n    List<List<int[]>> adj = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    for (int[] f : flights) adj.get(f[0]).add(new int[]{f[1], f[2]});\n    int[] fewest = new int[n];                           // fewest flights of any earlier pop of each city\n    Arrays.fill(fewest, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));   // [cost, city, used]\n    heap.add(new int[]{0, src, 0});\n    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n        int[] top = heap.poll();\n        int cost = top[0], city = top[1], used = top[2];\n        if (city == dst) return cost;                    // the cheapest route within the limit\n        if (used >= fewest[city] || used == k + 1) continue;   // dominated, or out of flights\n        fewest[city] = used;\n        for (int[] f : adj.get(city)) heap.add(new int[]{cost + f[1], f[0], used + 1});\n    }\n    return -1;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int findCheapestPrice(int n, vector<vector<int>>& flights, int src, int dst, int k) {\n    vector<vector<pair<int, int>>> adj(n);\n    for (auto& f : flights) adj[f[0]].push_back({f[1], f[2]});\n    vector<int> fewest(n, INT_MAX);                      // fewest flights of any earlier pop of each city\n    priority_queue<array<int, 3>, vector<array<int, 3>>, greater<>> heap;   // {cost, city, used}, cheapest first\n    heap.push({0, src, 0});\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        auto [cost, city, used] = heap.top(); heap.pop();\n        if (city == dst) return cost;                    // the cheapest route within the limit\n        if (used >= fewest[city] || used == k + 1) continue;   // dominated, or out of flights\n        fewest[city] = used;\n        for (auto [next, price] : adj[city]) heap.push({cost + price, next, used + 1});\n    }\n    return -1;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(k · E · log(k · E)), space O(k · E).** Each city can be expanded up to k + 1 times, each time with strictly fewer flights, so the heap holds at most about (k + 1) · E entries."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
